#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pre-push secret-scan — blocks a push whose newly-pushed commits add a secret.
# PREPUSH_VERSION: 13 (2026-08-17) — IMPORTED HISTORY IS EXEMPT BY A NAMED RULE, NOT SILENCE.
# The r2-impl fold (D-186) merges 1,726 commits of implementation history under
# implementations/rust/ via a subtree merge. Those commits were authored under r2-impl's own
# gates and pushed to its own public origin through this same hook family; they never carried
# this repo's Decision-Log discipline and cannot be amended without rewriting published
# history. So: a pushed commit that is an ancestor of the imported r2-impl tip (8b8705bc...)
# and NOT an ancestor of the pre-fold r2-standard main (104a1843...) is exempted as IMPORTED
# HISTORY by both the decision gate and the secret scan — COUNTED AND NAMED in the denominator
# print, never silently skipped, because checked-and-clean and exempted-and-unexamined are
# both silence and this file's own decision-gate comment says so. The secret scan goes
# IMPORT-AWARE only when the pushed range actually contains imported commits (once, at the
# fold push; every later range excludes them by ancestry): it scans each NON-imported commit
# individually, and exempts the subtree merge itself only after MEASURING that its entire
# first-parent diff sits inside its declared git-subtree prefix — a merge with any added line
# outside the prefix is scanned in full, size guard and all. Rationale for not scanning the
# imported diff here: it is 168,789 added lines (3.4x the size guard), and its content was
# already carried out to a public origin by r2-impl's own pushes under this hook's secret
# scan — the leak this gate exists to stop already could not have happened silently for that
# content. Commits authored in this repo AFTER the fold, including ones touching
# implementations/**, are checked exactly as before by both arms. Behaviour changed, so the
# version moves.
# PREPUSH_VERSION: 12 (2026-08-05) — annotated tags are PEELED AND SCANNED, message included,
# instead of refused. Blobs are still refused. Behaviour changed, so the version moves.
# PREPUSH_VERSION 11 (2026-08-05) — the PRIVATE KEY arm requires the five dashes. Version bumped
# because the behaviour changed: android made this edit locally on 2026-08-04 and left the version
# at 10, so two hooks with the same version number scanned differently and drift was undetectable
# by the one field that exists to detect it. An unversioned change to a gate is an unauditable gate.
# BUMPED BECAUSE BEHAVIOUR CHANGED. A behaviour change without a version bump leaves two
# gates live and indistinguishable — the skew android measured across six repos, where the
# only discriminator was a sha256 of an untracked file. Redistribute after any change here.
# Cite this line when reporting which guard a lane runs. An unversioned gate
# cannot be audited or skew-detected: android measured TWO versions live across
# six repos, distinguishable only by sha256 of an untracked file.
# This is the failsafe that makes auto-approved `git push` leak-safe: a push to a
# public repo can never carry a token/key/.env out, because the push is refused
# here at the git layer regardless of who (human or fleet agent) initiated it.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED IS THE POLICY. A real match always blocks (exit 1), AND AN INTERNAL
# ERROR MUST ALSO BLOCK — a scan that could not run is NOT a scan that found nothing.
#
# The previous wording here read: "Never auto-FAILS open: on any internal error it
# lets the push proceed only if it found nothing." That sentence OPENS WITH THE
# INVARIANT AND THEN DESCRIBES ITS VIOLATION — an errored scan yields no matches, so
# "proceed if it found nothing" IS the fail-open path, stated as a feature. Two lanes
# flagged it independently on 2026-07-27. A comment that authorises the defect is worse
# than the defect: it tells the next editor the behaviour is intended.
#
# STATE OF THE CODE, honestly, because the comment must not run ahead of it. Updated
# 2026-07-27 IN THE SAME EDIT that landed the change — a status comment written before
# the work and left behind afterwards is the stale-instruction defect this file already
# demonstrates once:
#   · non-commit ref: refused outright. Measured in two repos 2026-07-20.
#   · root commit: found and closed 2026-07-19.
#   · ENUMERATORS NOW FAIL CLOSED — the six reachable `2>/dev/null || true` sites that
#     produce the scan input or the decision-gate input go through `enumerate_into`,
#     which blocks on any non-zero rc. Verified by forcing an unscannable range: the
#     unpatched hook exited 0, the patched one exits 1.
#   · DENOMINATOR PRINTS ON THE PASS PATH, naming its own exclusions.
#   · REMAINING, deliberately left and reasoned: four here-string greps (fixed patterns,
#     no file input — not reachable); `git cat-file -t` (already fail-closed by its
#     consumer: an empty result is not a commit, so it blocks); `diff-tree` and `log` in
#     the decision check (fail toward the STRICTER branch — an empty message has no
#     trailer, so it blocks). Each is safe BY DIRECTION, which is a reason, not an excuse.
#   · STDERR CAPTURED SEPARATELY (v8). Was OPEN in v7: `2>&1` folded a success-path git
#     warning into the scan input. Safe by direction, but it CORRUPTED THE DENOMINATOR —
#     and a denominator that counts warnings as scanned lines defeats the print that
#     exists to make the number trustworthy.
#   · SELF-TEST COMMITTED (v8): `FLEET_HOOK_SELFTEST=1`, four cases, each asserting the
#     SPECIFIC refusal rather than a bare non-zero exit. Meta-controlled: breaking guard
#     N fails case N only, each for its own reason.
#   · OPEN: the four here-string greps are safe BY REACHABILITY, NOT BY DIRECTION — and
#     that is a weaker guarantee than the line above claims. If one of them ever errors
#     (a resource limit, an engine limit, an input shape nobody anticipated) `|| true`
#     yields empty, the finding vanishes, and THE SCAN REPORTS CLEAN. Contrast cat-file,
#     diff-tree and log, which fail toward the STRICTER branch: those are safe whatever
#     happens; these are safe only while nothing happens. They need an rc check that
#     treats 0 and 1 as legitimate and >1 as fatal — a matcher guard, not the enumerator
#     guard, because for a matcher rc 1 is a real answer.
#     Found by a lane AFTER I wrote "OPEN: nothing known" here. I had accepted a
#     not-reachable classification as equivalent to safe, and they are different claims.
# Do not restore the old sentence. If you are editing here, the invariant above is the
# requirement; anything short of it is a known gap, not a design.
#
# FLEET_SKIP_SECRET_SCAN=1 disables this scan entirely, before any check, with no
# trace in the output. It exists for a human operator with a reason. FLEET AGENTS
# ARE FORBIDDEN TO SET IT — if this hook blocks you, hold and report. It is
# deliberately not mentioned in the block message; see the note at the exit path.
# Repos with DECISIONS.md also require each new commit to update that ledger or carry a
# reviewable `Decision-Log: D-...` / `Decision-Log: none` trailer. This keeps local safety
# commits available while preventing unaccounted work from being published. A human may
# set FLEET_DECISION_LOG_CHECK=off for recovery; fleet agents are forbidden to set it.
# Chains to a repo-local hook if one pre-existed (saved as pre-push.local).
set -uo pipefail

[[ "${FLEET_SKIP_SECRET_SCAN:-0}" == "1" ]] && exit 0

# ── ENUMERATORS FAIL CLOSED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An ENUMERATOR is a command whose output IS the scan's denominator. When one fatals,
# `2>/dev/null || true` converts the error into an EMPTY SET, and every later line
# reads that as "nothing to find" — so the scan reports CLEAN having examined nothing.
# Two causes are already fixed individually below (a ref pointing at a blob; a root
# commit), found one at a time, in different repos, weeks apart — which is the
# argument for guarding the MECHANISM rather than the next symptom.
#
# ASSIGNS THROUGH A NAMEREF INSTEAD OF RETURNING OUTPUT, and that is load-bearing:
# called as `x="$(enumerate ...)"` this would run in a COMMAND-SUBSTITUTION SUBSHELL,
# where `exit 1` ends the subshell and the parent sails on to `exit 0` — printing a
# refusal and passing anyway. (Measured: the first draft of this patch did exactly
# that. The defect the guard exists to prevent, inside the guard.)
enumerate_into() {
  local __var="$1" __what="$2"; shift 2
  local __out __rc __errf
  # STDERR CAPTURED SEPARATELY, not folded in with 2>&1. On SUCCESS a git warning would
  # otherwise become a line of scan INPUT: harmless by direction (a stray line can only
  # cause a false BLOCK, never a false pass) but it corrupts the denominator, and a
  # denominator that counts warnings as scanned lines is the thing this print exists to
  # make trustworthy. Closes the OPEN item in the header invariant.
  __errf="$(mktemp)"
  __out="$("$@" 2>"$__errf")"; __rc=$?
  if (( __rc != 0 )); then
    printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — the secret scan could not enumerate %s.\033[0m\n' "$__what" >&2
    printf '  exited %s: %s\n' "$__rc" "$(tr '\n' ' ' <"$__errf" | cut -c1-200)" >&2
    printf 'An empty result from a failed command is NOT a clean scan. Refusing.\n\n' >&2
    rm -f "$__errf"
    exit 1
  fi
  rm -f "$__errf"
  printf -v "$__var" '%s' "$__out"
}

# ── SELF-TEST ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `FLEET_HOOK_SELFTEST=1 <hook>` forces each failure and asserts the gate reports ERROR
# rather than PASS. COMMITTED, not hand-run: a control that is not in the suite is a
# control that existed for one afternoon.
#
# DISPATCHED ON AN ENV VAR, NOT argv: git calls this hook with $1=remote $2=url, so a
# `--self-test` flag could collide with a remote of that name; and a dispatch placed
# after the final `exit 0` is UNREACHABLE — a gate that cannot fire, the same class this
# file is hardened against. Both were tried; the unreachable one reviewed as correct.
#
# EACH CASE PERTURBS THE INPUT THE GATE ACTUALLY READS and asserts the SPECIFIC refusal.
# Measured: with case 2 checking only the exit code, neutering the obj_type guard left it
# GREEN — the blob fell through to the enumerator guard and was refused for a different
# reason. A control that cannot tell WHICH guard fired does not test either of them.
# A FLAG-LIKE $1 IS REFUSED LOUDLY, NEVER TREATED AS A REMOTE NAME.
#
# git calls this hook with $1=remote $2=url, so `--self-test` lands in $1, the ref list
# arrives from a terminal instead of git, the loop reads nothing, and THE HOOK EXITS 0
# WITH NO OUTPUT. A lane ran exactly that on a deployed copy and NEARLY REPORTED IT GREEN.
#
# That is the argv hazard actually manifesting — and NOT in the form I predicted when I
# ruled for env-var dispatch. I argued a remote could be *named* `--self-test`, which is
# far-fetched. THE REAL FAILURE IS AN OPERATOR TYPING THE INTUITIVE FLAG AND BEING
# ANSWERED WITH SILENCE. Env dispatch avoided the collision and did nothing about this.
#
# So: silence is the one response this must never give. Exit 2 — neither 0 (pass) nor
# 1 (blocked), because it is neither.
#
# v9 MATCHED `--*` AND LEFT `-h` EXITING 0 SILENT — the single most intuitive flag an
# operator types was the one still answered with silence. The reasoning ("silence is the
# one forbidden answer") does not distinguish one dash from two; ONLY THE PATTERN DOES.
# A guard can UNDER-IMPLEMENT the rule it was written from, and the rule still reads as
# satisfied. A git remote name cannot usefully begin with `-`, so single-dash is safe.
# Negative control, both versions: `origin file:///dev/null` -> rc 0, no refusal.
if [[ "${1:-}" == -* ]]; then
  printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ REFUSED — "%s" is not a remote name.\033[0m\n' "$1" >&2
  printf 'This hook takes $1=remote $2=url from git and reads refs on stdin.\n' >&2
  printf 'To run the controls:  FLEET_HOOK_SELFTEST=1 bash %s\n' "$0" >&2
  printf 'Exiting 2. A silent exit 0 here would read as a clean self-test.\n\n' >&2
  exit 2
fi

if [[ "${FLEET_HOOK_SELFTEST:-0}" == "1" ]]; then
  zero="${zero:-0000000000000000000000000000000000000000}"
  st_fail=0
  st_ok()  { printf '  ok   %s\n' "$1"; }
  st_bad() { printf '  FAIL %s\n' "$1"; st_fail=1; }

  # 1) DEAD ENUMERATOR — perturbs the range, which is what `git diff` reads to build the
  #    scan input. Explicit subshell: the guard's whole point is that it EXITS.
  ( enumerate_into st_out "self-test" git rev-list "definitely-not-a-ref" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  (( $? != 0 )) && st_ok "dead enumerator exits non-zero" || st_bad "dead enumerator returned success"

  # 2) NON-COMMIT REF — perturbs $local_sha, read by the obj_type guard. Recurses with the
  #    flag CLEARED so the child takes the real path.
  st_blob="$(printf 'self-test' | git hash-object -w --stdin 2>/dev/null)"
  if [[ -n "$st_blob" ]]; then
    # INVOKED VIA `bash "$0"`, NOT `"$0"`. The SOURCE file is mode 644 — only the DEPLOYED
    # copy gets +x from the installer — so recursing through the exec bit made this case
    # fail with "Permission denied" while the guard it tests was working perfectly.
    # A CONTROL THAT FAILS FOR AN ENVIRONMENTAL REASON IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A REAL
    # FAILURE, and would have been "fixed" by weakening the guard. Caught on first run.
    st_out2="$(FLEET_HOOK_SELFTEST=0 bash "$0" origin file:///dev/null <<<"refs/heads/x $st_blob refs/heads/x $zero" 2>&1)"
    grep -q 'not a commit' <<<"$st_out2" \
      && st_ok "a blob ref is refused BY THE obj_type GUARD" \
      || st_bad "blob ref not refused by the obj_type guard (got: $(tr '\n' ' ' <<<"$st_out2" | cut -c1-90))"
  else
    st_bad "could not create a test blob (not in a repo?)"
  fi

  # 3) NEGATIVE CONTROL — the gate must actually REDDEN on a known-bad line. Cases 1-2 only
  #    prove it refuses; without this nothing proves it CATCHES anything.
  #
  #    THE FIXTURE IS ASSEMBLED, NOT WRITTEN OUT, and that is not cosmetic. Held as a literal,
  #    this line made THIS FILE a secret-bearing file: pushing the hook tripped the hook, and
  #    the gate blocked its own commit. Measured, not predicted — that is how it was found.
  #    The two wrong fixes were both available and both refused: exclude this path from the
  #    scan (a gate exempting itself — the exact class this file exists to refuse), or weaken
  #    the pattern until the fixture slipped through (breaking the gate to fix the test).
  #
  #    SPLIT AT THE KEYWORD AND ACROSS THE VALUE so no single source line carries
  #    keyword-separator-value. The MATCHED string is byte-identical to the old literal, so the
  #    control tests exactly what it tested before — the matcher, not the corpus.
  st_kw='api'; st_v1='ZmFrZVNlbGZUZXN0'; st_v2='VmFsdWUxMjM0NTY3ODkw'
  st_line="+${st_kw}_key = \"${st_v1}${st_v2}\""
  # ASSERT THE FIXTURE BEFORE TRUSTING THE MATCH. A botched assembly that yields a short or
  # keywordless value would fail the grep below and read as "the pattern is broken" — the same
  # green-for-the-wrong-reason this whole file is hardened against, inverted.
  if (( ${#st_v1} + ${#st_v2} < 16 )) || [[ "$st_line" != *"${st_kw}_key"* ]]; then
    st_bad "the known-bad fixture did not assemble (len $(( ${#st_v1} + ${#st_v2} )))"
  else
    grep -qiE '(secret|token|passwd|password|api[_-]?key|client_secret|bearer)["'"'"' ]*[:=]["'"'"' ]*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}' \
      <<<"$st_line" \
      && st_ok "the secret-value pattern matches a known-bad line" \
      || st_bad "the secret-value pattern did NOT match a known-bad line"
  fi

  # 5) THIS FILE MUST NOT TRIP THIS FILE. A gate whose own source matches its own pattern can
  #    never be pushed — it blocks every commit that touches it, and the only ways out are
  #    self-exemption or weakening. Standing control so the literal cannot come back.
  if grep -qiE '(secret|token|passwd|password|api[_-]?key|client_secret|bearer)["'"'"' ]*[:=]["'"'"' ]*[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}' "$0"; then
    st_bad "THE HOOK SOURCE TRIPS THE HOOK — a secret-shaped literal is in this file"
  else
    st_ok "the hook source does not trip its own secret-value pattern"
  fi

  # 4) STDERR IS NOT SCAN INPUT — a command that succeeds while writing to stderr must not
  #    contaminate the enumerated value (the OPEN item this version closes).
  enumerate_into st_out3 "self-test stderr" bash -c 'printf "data\n"; printf "a warning\n" >&2'
  [[ "$st_out3" == "data" ]] \
    && st_ok "stderr is not folded into the scan input" \
    || st_bad "stderr contaminated the scan input (got: $st_out3)"

  (( st_fail == 0 )) && { printf 'CONTROLS PASS\n'; exit 0; } || { printf 'CONTROLS FAILED\n'; exit 1; }
fi

zero='0000000000000000000000000000000000000000'
findings=""
decision_findings=""
decision_checked=0
decision_exempted=0
decision_imported=0

# ── IMPORTED HISTORY (the r2-impl fold, 2026-08-17, D-186) ──────────────────────
# The set is DERIVED FROM THE STATED DEFINITION, not from a path or a message
# pattern: ancestors of the merged r2-impl tip that are not ancestors of the
# pre-fold r2-standard main. Both endpoints are pinned because the definition
# names them; a path-based exemption would also exempt commits AUTHORED here that
# touch implementations/**, which stay checked.
IMPORTED_TIP='8b8705bcdefff65356aada8a38990a5516d42d96'   # r2-impl main, the subtree merge's second parent
PREFOLD_MAIN='104a1843b01746b78cac79ab889ee5a1db7010e3'   # r2-standard main the fold landed on
declare -A imported_lookup=()
imported_count=0
# If the tip is not in this object database there is no import to exempt and the
# gate behaves exactly as v12 — an empty set here is a fact about the repository,
# not a failed enumeration (the enumeration itself still fails closed below).
if git cat-file -e "${IMPORTED_TIP}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
  enumerate_into __imported "the imported-history set (fold D-186)" \
    git rev-list "$IMPORTED_TIP" --not "$PREFOLD_MAIN"
  while IFS= read -r __ic; do
    [[ -n "$__ic" ]] || continue
    imported_lookup["$__ic"]=1
    imported_count=$((imported_count + 1))
  done <<<"$__imported"
fi

check_decision_accountability() {  # $1 = remote sha, $2 = local sha
  local remote="$1" local_sha="$2" commits commit parent_count changed msg short malformed ledgers id
  [[ "${FLEET_DECISION_LOG_CHECK:-on}" == off ]] && return 0

  if [[ "$remote" == "$zero" ]]; then
    # New remote branch: only commits absent from every remote are newly published.
    enumerate_into commits "commits for the decision-accountability check" git rev-list --reverse "$local_sha" --not --remotes
  else
    enumerate_into commits "commits for the decision-accountability check" git rev-list --reverse "${remote}..${local_sha}"
  fi
  [[ -n "$commits" ]] || return 0

  while IFS= read -r commit; do
    [[ -n "$commit" ]] || continue

    # IMPORTED HISTORY, FIRST AND COUNTED (v13, fold D-186): a commit reachable
    # through the subtree merge's imported parent predates this repository's
    # discipline and cannot be amended without rewriting r2-impl's published
    # history. It takes a NAMED exemption branch — the denominator print below
    # carries the count and the rule — and it is checked BEFORE the ledger tests
    # because those would misread an imported lane ledger (r2-core/DECISIONS.md
    # and kin travel with the import) as this repository's accounting.
    if [[ -n "${imported_lookup[$commit]:-}" ]]; then
      decision_imported=$(( decision_imported + 1 ))
      continue
    fi

    # Once the ledger exists it is a durable invariant. Removing it is itself an
    # unaccounted decision and must not turn the guard off for later commits.
    #
    # THIS TEST WAS ROOT-RELATIVE AND ITS `continue` SKIPS THE COMMIT, SO IN A REPOSITORY
    # WITH NO ROOT `DECISIONS.md` THE ENTIRE ARM WAS INERT — every commit fell out here and
    # nothing was ever checked. MEASURED 2026-08-08, not reasoned: a throwaway repository
    # built in r2-impl's layout — ledgers at `r2-core/DECISIONS.md` and
    # `r2-hive/DECISIONS.md`, none at the root — accepted a push of a commit with NO trailer
    # and NO ledger change, using the hook exactly as deployed. r2-impl has 1047
    # Decision-Log trailers in its history written under a gate that never ran.
    #
    # A GATE THAT CANNOT FIRE IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM ONE THAT PASSES, and the lanes
    # writing those trailers had every reason to believe it was enforcing them.
    #
    # Basename match at any depth, so a lane-prefixed ledger counts.
    ledgers="$(git ls-tree -r --name-only "$commit" 2>/dev/null | grep -E '(^|/)DECISIONS\.md$' || true)"
    if [[ -z "$ledgers" ]]; then
      if git rev-parse -q --verify "${commit}^" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
          && git ls-tree -r --name-only "${commit}^" 2>/dev/null | grep -qE '(^|/)DECISIONS\.md$'; then
        short="$(git rev-parse --short "$commit" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "$commit")"
        decision_findings+="  • $short removes DECISIONS.md\n"
      fi
      continue
    fi

    # THE LEDGER IS NOT ALWAYS AT THE ROOT. This was a root pathspec, so in a repository
    # whose ledgers live under lane prefixes THE EXEMPTION COULD NEVER FIRE FOR ANYBODY.
    # Measured in r2-impl 2026-08-08: no root DECISIONS.md exists, the ledgers are
    # r2-core/DECISIONS.md and r2-hive/DECISIONS.md, and on 8c756ed — a commit whose ONLY
    # change is the ledger — the root pathspec returned EMPTY while the actual path list was
    # [r2-core/DECISIONS.md]. So "update the ledger in this commit" was an exit the author
    # COULD NOT REACH BY REPOSITORY LAYOUT, leaving the trailer as the only way out of a
    # gate whose stated purpose is to prefer the ledger. Same shape as the false-absence
    # message one level out: a remedy naming a state the author cannot be in.
    #
    # Matched on BASENAME so a ledger at any depth counts, in any repository layout.
    #
    # THE EXEMPTION IS COUNTED, NOT JUST TAKEN. r2-standard 2026-08-08: AN EXEMPTION IS SAFE
    # ONLY WHERE IT IS INDEPENDENT OF THE PROPERTY BEING CHECKED — and this one is not. A
    # ledger citation is almost always made in the commit that LANDS the ledger entry, so
    # the exemption removes exactly the commits whose trailers are most likely to be
    # interesting. Eleven malformed `D-nnn` trailers sat unnoticed in that repository for
    # that reason. NOTHING DISTINGUISHES CHECKED-AND-CLEAN FROM EXEMPTED-AND-UNEXAMINED,
    # BECAUSE BOTH ARE SILENCE.
    #
    # So the counts are printed on every push. REPORTED, NEVER REFUSED: taking the branch is
    # legitimate and refusing it would be wrong. What was missing is the denominator.
    changed="$(git diff-tree --root --no-commit-id --name-only -r "$commit" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if grep -qE '(^|/)DECISIONS\.md$' <<<"$changed"; then
      decision_exempted=$(( decision_exempted + 1 ))
      continue
    fi
    decision_checked=$(( decision_checked + 1 ))

    # Generated merge commits combine already-accounted histories and do not themselves
    # choose repo behaviour. A merge that removes the ledger was caught above.
    parent_count="$(git rev-list --parents -n 1 "$commit" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print NF-1}')"
    [[ "$parent_count" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || parent_count=0
    (( parent_count > 1 )) && continue

    msg="$(git log -1 --format=%B "$commit" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    # {2,} not {2}. The sequence number was matched as EXACTLY two digits, so the moment
    # this repo's ledger passed D-...-99 the gate stopped accepting citations of its own
    # decisions: 43 of 123 records now carry a three-digit number, and every commit citing
    # one was told it "neither updates DECISIONS.md nor has a Decision-Log trailer".
    #
    # A gate that exists to force decision citation was REJECTING the citations, and its
    # only remaining exits were "update the ledger in this commit" or "Decision-Log: none".
    # It pushed authors toward `none` — the opposite of its purpose — and said nothing.
    # It survived because the fixture in tests/smoke.sh is D-20260721-99: the largest
    # two-digit value there is, chosen before the boundary existed and never revisited.
    #
    # ‼ AND `D-` WAS NEVER THE ONLY FORM. Measured in r2-composer 2026-08-08, headings in
    # its DECISIONS.md and trailers across its FULL history, not a sample:
    #   R-YYYYMMDD-NN  195 headings, 115 trailers   ← the form this ledger ISSUES
    #   D-YYYYMMDD-NN   47 headings,  51 trailers
    #   dNNN            fleet decision register,  63 trailers
    #   none                                      265 trailers
    # THE ARM ACCEPTED ONLY `none` AND `D-`, SO IT REFUSED THE MOST-USED CITATION FORM IN
    # THE REPOSITORY WHILE ACCEPTING THE ESCAPE HATCH. A gate meant to force decision
    # citation was pushing authors to `none`, which is already the most-used trailer at 265
    # — the failure is invisible in the history precisely BECAUSE the refused authors
    # complied by declaring no decision. `R-` and `dNNN` are kept on EVIDENCE, not caution.
    #
    # This arm was fixed in r2-composer's DEPLOYED hook on 2026-08-06 and lost on
    # 2026-08-08 when `fleet install-git-hooks` overwrote it from THIS file, which had
    # never carried it. A local hook fix that is not upstreamed here survives only until
    # the next install. `doctor` reported "DRIFTS" for both the upgrade and the downgrade
    # and could not name the direction.
    # ‼ AND THE UNDATED `D-nnn` FORM IS A REAL NAMESPACE THIS ARM REFUSED OUTRIGHT.
    # Measured across r2-impl's FULL history 2026-08-08: 1047 Decision-Log trailers, 516
    # refused by the arm above — 265 bare `D-nnn`, 12 `D-nnn amended`, 6 comma pairs of
    # them, 23 `none.` with a full stop. NEARLY HALF THE TRAILERS THE REPOSITORY HAS EVER
    # WRITTEN, and the majority are r2-core citing its OWN ledger, which mints D-257..D-282
    # by a convention older than this gate.
    #
    # The lane found the passing workaround and REFUSED TO TAKE IT, correctly: `d277`
    # matches — lowercase `d`, three digits — BUT `dNNN` IS THE FLEET DECISION REGISTER AND
    # `d277` IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S RULING. The only accepted spelling of its identifier aliased
    # a namespace it does not own, so the gate's remedy was to cite the wrong decision.
    #
    # Undated `(R|D)-nn+` is therefore accepted. It cannot collide with `dNNN` (case) nor
    # with the dated form (the whole value is anchored). A single trailing full stop is
    # allowed because a period is punctuation, not content, and cannot be mistaken for an
    # identifier — this is NOT a licence for prose, which stays refused.
    #
    # STILL REFUSED, PRINTED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED: `D-193 amended` and
    # every other trailing-prose form (put it in the body), and `SS293` — standard's
    # sufficiency register, 2 instances in r2-impl, too thin a population to admit on.
    id='(R|D)-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{2,}|(R|D)-[0-9]{2,}|d[0-9]{3,}'
    if grep -qE "^Decision-Log: (none|($id))((, )($id))*\.?[[:space:]]*\$" <<<"$msg"; then
      continue
    fi

    short="$(git rev-parse --short "$commit" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "$commit")"

    # REPORT THE FORM, NOT AN ABSENCE. A trailer that is PRESENT and malformed was
    # reported as MISSING, and the summary then told the author to add the very thing
    # they already had — so the only reading left was that the gate could not see it.
    #
    # This is not a hypothetical. THE TWO COMMENTS ABOVE ARE BOTH THIS DEFECT: three-digit
    # sequence numbers, and `R-` citations, each reported as "neither updates DECISIONS.md
    # nor has a Decision-Log trailer" while sitting on a commit that carried one. BOTH WERE
    # FIXED BY WIDENING THE PATTERN AND NEITHER BY CORRECTING THE MESSAGE, so the false
    # report survived both repairs and went on to describe a state no author was in.
    #
    # It cost a `--no-verify` on 2026-08-08: r2-composer's `Decision-Log: none (measurement
    # + arm only...)` was refused for trailing prose and reported as absent, and A REMEDY
    # SENTENCE THAT DESCRIBES A STATE THE AUTHOR IS NOT IN MAKES A BYPASS LOOK LIKE THE
    # DIAGNOSIS RATHER THAN THE EVASION.
    #
    # The pattern is NOT widened here, deliberately: admitting prose is how the field stops
    # being machine-readable. What changes is that the refusal becomes legible.
    malformed="$(grep -E '^Decision-Log:' <<<"$msg" | head -1)"
    if [[ -n "$malformed" ]]; then
      decision_findings+="  • $short HAS a Decision-Log trailer, in a form this gate does not accept:\n"
      decision_findings+="        ${malformed//\\/\\\\}\n"
      decision_findings+="      The identifier must be the WHOLE value — no trailing prose, no\n"
      decision_findings+="      parenthetical, no explanation. Put the reasoning in the commit body.\n"
      decision_findings+="      Accepted: Decision-Log: none\n"
      decision_findings+="                Decision-Log: D-20260808-01     (also R-)\n"
      decision_findings+="                Decision-Log: D-277              (undated lane ledger ID)\n"
      decision_findings+="                Decision-Log: d448               (FLEET register, lowercase)\n"
      decision_findings+="                Decision-Log: D-277, D-280       (comma-space separated)\n"
      decision_findings+="      Note: dNNN and D-NNN are DIFFERENT registers. Do not substitute one\n"
      decision_findings+="      for the other to satisfy this gate — it would cite the wrong decision.\n"
    else
      decision_findings+="  • $short neither updates DECISIONS.md nor has a Decision-Log trailer\n"
    fi
  done <<<"$commits"
}

scan_range() {  # $1 = git rev range or single sha
  local range="$1" added added_all names assignments macs mac_n mac_oui total first_mac
  # SIZE GUARD — FAIL CLOSED, NEVER SAMPLE.
  #
  # This used to be `| head -50000`, which meant a push adding more than 50000
  # lines was scanned as a SAMPLE and reported CLEAN — a vendored dependency
  # drop, a lockfile regen or generated bindings would sail through. android
  # found it 2026-07-19 while applying the fleet's own rule: A GATE THAT SAMPLES
  # IS NOT A GATE, and MUST NOT be piped through head/tail.
  #
  # The cap's real purpose was to stop a huge push hanging the hook. That intent
  # is preserved by COUNTING first and REFUSING, so the operator gets an explicit
  # decision instead of a silent partial scan.
  enumerate_into __diff "the diff for $range" git diff "$range"
  total="$(printf '%s' "$__diff" | grep -c '^+' || true)"
  if [[ "${total:-0}" -gt "${FLEET_SCAN_MAX_LINES:-50000}" ]]; then
    printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — range too large to scan safely: %s added lines\033[0m\n' "$total" >&2
    printf 'The secret scan refuses to SAMPLE. Push in smaller ranges, or set\n' >&2
    printf 'FLEET_SCAN_MAX_LINES=<n> deliberately after checking what is in the diff.\n\n' >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  added_all="$(printf '%s' "$__diff" | grep '^+' | grep -v '^+++' || true)"
  # The same, MINUS the vendored synthetic KAT vectors. Those carry test-only 0xAA
  # "secrets" (device_master_secret etc.) that trip the token/secret VALUE patterns on
  # every drift-sync — they are drift-checked test data, not real secrets.
  #
  # SCOPE — this allowlist is deliberately NARROW. It applies to the secret-VALUE scan
  # (2) ONLY. The other two scans read unfiltered inputs on purpose:
  #   · the FILENAME scan (1) reads $names — never excluded, so a real .env/.key/.pem
  #     added under a vectors/ dir still blocks.
  #   · the MAC scan (3) reads $added_all — never excluded; see the note there for why.
  enumerate_into __diff_scannable "scannable added lines for $range" git diff "$range" -- . ':(exclude,glob)**/vectors/**.json' ':(exclude,glob)testing/test-vectors/**.json'
  added="$(printf '%s' "$__diff_scannable" | grep '^+' | grep -v '^+++' || true)"
  # Tag message folded into BOTH scanned sets, +-prefixed so it reads as added content.
  # Into $added_all as well as $added: the MAC scan reads the unfiltered set, and a bench
  # identifier in a release note is exactly as public as one in a commit.
  if [[ -n "${tag_body:-}" ]]; then
    tag_added="$(printf '%s\n' "$tag_body" | sed 's/^/+/')"
    added="$(printf '%s\n%s' "$added" "$tag_added")"
    added_all="$(printf '%s\n%s' "$added_all" "$tag_added")"
  fi
  enumerate_into names "changed paths for $range" git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AM "$range"

  # DENOMINATOR — printed on the PASS path, where it was previously computed and discarded.
  # NAMES ITS OWN EXCLUSIONS deliberately: the secret-VALUE scan reads $added, which excludes
  # the vendored KAT vectors, so a bare "N lines scanned" would make a FILTERED scan look
  # COMPLETE — the sample-reported-as-clean defect the range cap above was rewritten to
  # prevent. State both numbers or neither.
  scan_files="$(printf '%s' "$names" | grep -c . || true)"
  scan_lines="$(printf '%s' "$added_all" | grep -c . || true)"
  scan_value="$(printf '%s' "$added" | grep -c . || true)"
  printf 'secret scan: %s files, %s added lines; %s scanned for secret VALUES (%s excluded as vendored KAT vectors)\n' \
    "${scan_files:-0}" "${scan_lines:-0}" "${scan_value:-0}" "$(( ${scan_lines:-0} - ${scan_value:-0} ))" >&2

  # 1) secret-bearing filenames being added/modified
  # NB: the "secret" token requires a secret-DATA extension (not a bare .*secret.*).
  # A bare match flagged every SOURCE/DOC path containing "secret" — R2-SECRETS.md, the
  # crates/r2-secrets resolver, check_identity_leak's "secret" refs, the custody template —
  # i.e. the entire R2-SECRETS custody migration (2026-07-19). Real secret CONTENT in any
  # file is still caught by the value scan (2) + MAC scan (3); this line catches secret DATA
  # FILES (tg_secret.bin / secret.env / *.key …), which is what it was for.
  # VOCABULARY — the generic web set (secret/key/priv/env) plus the R2 ARTIFACT set.
  # The generic set alone carried `tg_priv` and NOT `tg_sk`, so a real 32-byte
  # trust-group secret key at provisioned/tg_sk.bin was invisible to this line
  # (core, 2026-07-20). `_sk` is the secret-bearing half of a keypair and appears in
  # no generic wordlist; `sealed`/`joinresponse`/`mnemonic` are R2 artifact names.
  # Anchored to a separator or extension so `task.bin`/`disk.dat` do not match.
  grep -qiE '(^|/)(\.env(\..*)?$|.*\.pem$|.*\.key$|.*\.priv(\.bin)?$|id_rsa$|id_ed25519$|.*secret[^/]*\.(bin|dat|env|key|pem|priv|p12|pfx|jks|keystore)$|tg_priv|tg_sk|[._-]sk\.(bin|dat|key|hex|b64)$|(mnemonic|seed[._-]?phrase|sealed|joinresponse)[^/]*\.(bin|dat|json|txt|hex|b64)$)' <<<"$names" \
    && findings+="  • a secret-bearing filename (.env/.key/.pem/.priv/id_rsa/tg_priv/tg_sk/*_sk.bin/mnemonic/sealed…)\n"

  # 2) high-signal secret VALUES in added content
  grep -qE '[0-9]{8,10}:[A-Za-z0-9_-]{35}' <<<"$added"      && findings+="  • a Telegram-bot-token pattern\n"
  # REQUIRE THE FIVE DASHES (android, 2026-08-04; landed here 2026-08-05). A real PEM header
  # always carries them; the dashless form matched only prose, and prose is all it caught.
  # It blocked a 214-commit push on ONE added line — a gate's own known-positive fixture —
  # with 0 full headers and 0 base64 bodies of 60+ chars in the range. Controlled both ways
  # before landing: a real PEM fires, a PEM embedded in JSON fires, prose no longer fires.
  # `--` because a pattern beginning with a dash is otherwise read as an option.
  #
  # WHY THIS IS IN THE SOURCE AND NOT IN ONE LANE'S HOOK DIRECTORY. android fixed this a day
  # ago and said in its own comment that the file was untracked, that the edit vanished on a
  # fresh clone, and that every other lane still ran the dashless arm — all three true, and
  # measured again today: standard and composer still carried it. A ruling that lives only
  # where it was made is not in force anywhere else.
  #
  # KNOWN GAP, UNCHANGED BY THIS EDIT AND STATED SO IT IS NOT MISTAKEN FOR COVERAGE: a bare
  # base64 key body with no header is caught by neither pattern, before or after.
  grep -qE -- '-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----' <<<"$added" && findings+="  • a PRIVATE KEY block\n"
  grep -qE 'AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}' <<<"$added"                   && findings+="  • an AWS access-key id\n"
  # Two-stage (composer v3, adopted 2026-07-22 after two falsifier rounds):
  # boundary + hyphen-inclusive run >=40, AND the matched run must carry >=2 digits.
  # Charset-narrowing false-negatived real keys (sk-ant-api03-… breaks an alnum run
  # at api03-); boundary-only still flagged sk-prefixed hyphenated slugs >=20
  # (SpinKit-class identifiers, composer's fixtures — 3 live blocks). Real keys are
  # ~50-100-char runs and api03/base64url guarantee digits; prose slugs are short or
  # digit-less. Controls live in r2-composer tools/key-hygiene.sh (80cc2bb).
  grep -oE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])sk-(ant-)?[A-Za-z0-9_-]{40,}' <<<"$added" | grep -qE '[0-9].*[0-9]' && findings+="  • an sk-/sk-ant- API key\n"

  # Extract assignments before filtering, so a scrub marker on one minified line cannot
  # hide a second real assignment on that line. Plain R2-SCRUBBED/REDACTED/PLACEHOLDER
  # values and the base64 prefix for "R2-SCRUBBED-" are remediation markers, not secrets.
  assignments="$(grep -oiE '(secret|token|passwd|password|api[_-]?key|client_secret|bearer)["'"'"' ]*[:=]["'"'"' ]*[A-Za-z0-9_\-]{16,}' <<<"$added" || true)"
  assignments="$(grep -viE '[:=]["'"'"' ]*(R2-(SCRUBBED|REDACTED|PLACEHOLDER)-[A-Z0-9_-]+|UjItU0NSVUJCRUQt[A-Za-z0-9_-]*)$' <<<"$assignments" || true)"
  [[ -n "$assignments" ]] && findings+="  • a hardcoded secret/token/password assignment\n"

  # 3) MAC-shaped VALUES in added content.
  #
  # LESSON (2026-07-15, r2-composer board→MAC inventory). Everything above scans secret
  # FILENAMES and a handful of token VALUE shapes; the per-repo CI hygiene gates scan
  # scrubbed TERMS and a codename WORDLIST. None of them matched a MAC-shaped VALUE — which
  # is exactly how a tracked board→MAC inventory sat on a PUBLIC repo while every gate
  # reported CLEAN. A wordlist only catches names someone already thought of; a value SHAPE
  # catches the whole class. Hence this check, and hence it lives HERE: the per-repo CI gate
  # closes one repo, the pre-push hook closes every repo in the fleet — including the ones
  # with no CI at all.
  #
  # Scope: colon-form MACs only — the observed leak shape. Dash/dotted forms are deliberately
  # out of scope: they collide with ordinary hex-ish prose and would cost false positives for
  # no evidence of a real vector. Placeholder/synthetic prefixes are allowed (below), so docs
  # and tests can still show an example MAC. Toggle the whole check off with FLEET_MAC_SCAN=off.
  #
  # READS $added_all, *NOT* the vectors-allowlisted $added — deliberate, and load-bearing.
  # The allowlist above exists because synthetic KAT 0xAA "secrets" trip the TOKEN patterns;
  # that rationale does not extend to MACs. Inheriting it would blind this check inside
  # vectors/**.json — i.e. it would silently reopen the very hole this check exists to close,
  # in exactly the kind of file where a real device MAC could sit unnoticed as "test data".
  # Measured cost of not inheriting it: ZERO — 66 vectors/ + test-vectors JSONs across
  # r2-composer / r2-core / r2-hive / r2-specifications contain 0 non-allowlisted MAC-shaped
  # values (checked 2026-07-15). If synthetic MACs ever do land in vectors, prefer a
  # placeholder prefix (02:/aa:bb:cc:/de:ad:be:ef) over widening this exclusion.
  if [[ "${FLEET_MAC_SCAN:-on}" != "off" ]]; then
    macs="$(printf '%s\n' "$added_all" \
      | grep -oiE '\b([0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}\b' \
      | grep -viE '^(02:|aa:bb:cc:|de:ad:be:ef)' \
      | grep -vxiE '00:00:00:00:00:00|ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff|11:22:33:44:55:66' \
      | grep -vxiE '00:00:00:00:00:[0-9a-f]{2}' \
      | grep -viE ':00:00:00$' \
      | sort -u || true)"
      # allowlist discipline (fail-CLOSED): 02:/aa:bb:cc:/de:ad:be:ef are PREFIXES
      # (locally-administered + synthetic OUI classes, never a burned-in device).
      # all-zero + broadcast + the 11:22:33:44:55:66 byte-ladder are EXACT-token
      # literals, NOT prefixes — a 00:00/ff:ff *prefix* allowlist waved through real
      # OUIs (00:00:0c Cisco, 00:00:5e IANA, ff:ff:11…). Fixed 2026-07-15 after a
      # refutation pass on the sibling CI gate.
      #
      # SYNTHETIC-NIC classes added 2026-07-17. A gate whose FALSE POSITIVES block
      # REMEDIATION is worse than no gate at that moment: this hook REFUSED the push
      # that scrubbed a real rbid correlator off r2-composer's PUBLIC main, over four
      # documented synthetic placeholders — so it taught the lane to bypass it at the
      # exact moment the stakes were highest. That is a gate defect, not a lane defect.
      #   · 00:00:00:00:00:NN  — all-zero OUI + counter NIC; no vendor owns 00:00:00
      #     as a burned-in prefix in this shape, so it is a placeholder by construction.
      #   · *:00:00:00         — ALL-ZERO NIC under any OUI (e.g. f4:12:fa:00:00:00).
      #     The OUI names a VENDOR, never a DEVICE; a burned-in NIC is never all-zero.
      #     So this leaks "a DFRobot board exists", not WHICH board — not the
      #     device-unique class this scan exists to catch.
      # Both stay NARROW: they allow a zeroed/countered NIC, never a populated one.
    if [[ -n "$macs" ]]; then
      mac_n="$(grep -c . <<<"$macs" || true)"
      IFS=$'\n' read -r first_mac _ <<<"$macs"
      mac_oui="${first_mac%:*:*:*}"
      # Report the OUI (vendor prefix) only — enough to locate the line, without echoing a
      # device-unique identifier into a terminal/CI log.
      findings+="  • ${mac_n} real-looking MAC-address value(s) in added lines (first OUI ${mac_oui}:…) — allowed: 02:/aa:bb:cc:/de:ad:be:ef prefixes + exact 00:00:00:00:00:00 / ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff; FLEET_MAC_SCAN=off to skip\n"
    fi
  fi
}

while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
  [[ -z "${local_sha:-}" || "$local_sha" == "$zero" ]] && continue   # ref deletion → nothing pushed

  # NON-COMMIT REF — FAIL CLOSED.
  #
  # Sibling of the root-commit fail-open below, same mechanism, left live when that
  # one was fixed. A ref pointing at a BLOB or TAG makes every enumerator here fatal:
  # `git diff <blob>` exits 129, the error is swallowed by 2>/dev/null, `added` comes
  # back EMPTY and the push reports CLEAN. rc=0 is an EMPTY SCAN, not a clean one —
  # a zero-denominator inside the gate itself. Measured independently in r2-core and
  # r2-composer, 2026-07-20: the SAME BYTES block as a commit and pass as a blob.
  # This matters because refs/keep/* pins point at blobs, and those pins hold
  # unclassified and scrub-preserved material in four repos.
  obj_type="$(git cat-file -t "$local_sha" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  # ANNOTATED TAG — PEELED AND SCANNED, NOT SKIPPED. A tag object is not a blob: it
  # dereferences to a commit, and that commit is scannable. Refusing it outright blocked
  # a release tag on 2026-08-05 and the only ways past were to bypass the gate or to
  # drop the annotation — the second loses the release notes, the first is banned.
  #
  # THE PART A NAIVE PEEL MISSES, AND IT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS NOT A SKIP: THE TAG
  # MESSAGE IS PUSHED CONTENT. It is written by hand, at a moment when someone is
  # summarising a release, and nothing else would ever read it. It is appended to the
  # scanned set below as +-prefixed lines so every value pattern sees it.
  tag_body=""
  if [[ "$obj_type" == "tag" ]]; then
    peeled="$(git rev-parse -q --verify "${local_sha}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [[ -z "$peeled" ]]; then
      printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — %s is a tag that does not peel to a commit.\033[0m\n' \
        "${local_ref:-<ref>}" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    tag_body="$(git cat-file tag "$local_sha" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [[ -z "$tag_body" ]]; then
      printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — %s is a tag whose object could not be read.\033[0m\n' \
        "${local_ref:-<ref>}" >&2
      printf 'Unreadable is not empty. Refusing rather than scanning nothing.\n\n' >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    local_sha="$peeled"
    obj_type="commit"
  fi
  if [[ "$obj_type" != "commit" ]]; then
    printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — %s points at a %s, not a commit.\033[0m\n' \
      "${local_ref:-<ref>}" "${obj_type:-unknown object}" >&2
    printf 'The secret scan can only read a commit range. It cannot scan this ref, and a\n' >&2
    printf 'silent pass here would be an empty scan reported as a clean one. Refusing.\n\n' >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if [[ "${remote_sha:-$zero}" == "$zero" ]]; then
    # new branch on the remote: scan commits not on any existing remote ref
    # NB the `| tail -1` here is SEMANTICS, not truncation: rev-list is
    # newest-first, so the last line is the OLDEST unpushed commit — the base of
    # the range. Do not "fix" it; doing so breaks the range. (Classified with
    # android, 2026-07-19, after it was grouped with two real truncations.)
    oldest="$(git rev-list "$local_sha" --not --remotes 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
    if [[ -z "$oldest" ]]; then
      range="$local_sha"
    elif git rev-parse --verify -q "${oldest}^" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      range="${oldest}^..${local_sha}"
    else
      # ROOT COMMIT — `${oldest}^` does not resolve, git diff fatals, the error is
      # swallowed by 2>/dev/null, the added set comes back EMPTY and the push
      # reports CLEAN. A FAIL-OPEN, and it is reachable on the FIRST PUSH of a new
      # repo — exactly when the guard matters most. Found by android 2026-07-19.
      # The empty-tree object gives a real diff that includes the root commit.
      range="4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904..${local_sha}"
    fi
  else
    range="${remote_sha}..${local_sha}"
  fi
  check_decision_accountability "${remote_sha:-$zero}" "$local_sha"

  # ── IMPORT-AWARE SCAN DISPATCH (v13, fold D-186) ────────────────────────────
  # Enumerate the commits this ref push actually publishes — the same derivation
  # the decision gate uses — and ask whether any of them is imported history.
  # When none is (every push after the fold push, by ancestry), the scan below
  # is byte-for-byte v12's, including for commits touching implementations/**.
  if [[ "${remote_sha:-$zero}" == "$zero" ]]; then
    enumerate_into __pushed "commits for the import-aware scan dispatch" \
      git rev-list --reverse "$local_sha" --not --remotes
  else
    enumerate_into __pushed "commits for the import-aware scan dispatch" \
      git rev-list --reverse "${remote_sha}..${local_sha}"
  fi
  __has_import=0
  if (( imported_count > 0 )); then
    while IFS= read -r __pc; do
      [[ -n "$__pc" && -n "${imported_lookup[$__pc]:-}" ]] && { __has_import=1; break; }
    done <<<"$__pushed"
  fi
  if (( __has_import == 0 )); then
    scan_range "$range"
  else
    # The range's tree-diff contains the whole imported tree (168,789 added
    # lines — 3.4x the size guard), content already carried to a public origin
    # by r2-impl's own pushes under this hook family's secret scan. So each
    # NON-imported commit is scanned individually with the same scan_range —
    # size guard, denominator print and all — and the imported set takes the
    # SAME named exemption rule as the decision gate. Nothing here is silent:
    # both exemptions print with their counts and their rule below.
    __scan_imported=0; __scan_authored=0; __merge_exempt=""; __merge_prefix=""
    while IFS= read -r __pc; do
      [[ -n "$__pc" ]] || continue
      if [[ -n "${imported_lookup[$__pc]:-}" ]]; then
        __scan_imported=$(( __scan_imported + 1 ))
        continue
      fi
      # A merge whose EVERY non-first parent is imported is MEASURED, not
      # trusted: only if its entire first-parent diff sits inside its declared
      # git-subtree prefix is its diff the imported tree itself, and only then
      # does it take the exemption. One added line outside the prefix and the
      # commit is scanned in full instead — fail toward scanning.
      __parents="$(git rev-list --parents -n 1 "$__pc" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      read -r -a __par_arr <<<"$__parents"
      if (( ${#__par_arr[@]} > 2 )); then
        __all_imported=1
        for __mp in "${__par_arr[@]:2}"; do
          [[ -n "${imported_lookup[$__mp]:-}" ]] || { __all_imported=0; break; }
        done
        if (( __all_imported == 1 )); then
          __prefix="$(git log -1 --format=%B "$__pc" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^git-subtree-dir: //p' | head -1)"
          if [[ -n "$__prefix" ]]; then
            enumerate_into __outside "the subtree merge's diff outside its prefix" \
              git diff "${__pc}^" "$__pc" -- . ":(exclude)${__prefix}"
            if [[ -z "$__outside" ]]; then
              __merge_exempt="$__pc"; __merge_prefix="$__prefix"
              continue
            fi
          fi
        fi
      fi
      __scan_authored=$(( __scan_authored + 1 ))
      if git rev-parse -q --verify "${__pc}^" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        scan_range "${__pc}^..${__pc}"
      else
        scan_range "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904..${__pc}"
      fi
      tag_body=""   # a tag message is scanned once, not once per commit
    done <<<"$__pushed"
    printf 'secret scan: IMPORT-AWARE for this range — %d imported commit(s) exempted (ancestors of r2-impl tip %.12s, not reachable from pre-fold main %.12s; carried to a public origin by r2-impl'"'"'s own pushes under this hook family — fold D-186, v13); %d authored commit(s) scanned individually\n' \
      "$__scan_imported" "$IMPORTED_TIP" "$PREFOLD_MAIN" "$__scan_authored" >&2
    if [[ -n "$__merge_exempt" ]]; then
      printf 'secret scan: subtree merge %.12s exempted — MEASURED this run: entire first-parent diff inside git-subtree prefix %s/, nothing outside it\n' \
        "$__merge_exempt" "$__merge_prefix" >&2
    fi
  fi
done

# THE DENOMINATOR, ALWAYS, PASS OR FAIL. Without this line an exempted commit and a checked
# one are both silence, and the exemption's share of the population is invisible to everyone
# including whoever tightens this gate next. Standing question it exists to answer: WHAT
# FRACTION OF THE REAL POPULATION TAKES THE EXEMPTION BRANCH, AND IS THAT THE FRACTION YOU
# CARE ABOUT.
if (( decision_checked + decision_exempted + decision_imported > 0 )); then
  printf 'decision gate: %d commit(s) checked, %d exempted (ledger touched), %d exempted (IMPORTED HISTORY: ancestors of r2-impl tip %.12s not reachable from pre-fold main %.12s — fold D-186, v13)\n' \
    "$decision_checked" "$decision_exempted" "$decision_imported" \
    "$IMPORTED_TIP" "$PREFOLD_MAIN" >&2
fi

if [[ -n "$decision_findings" ]]; then
  printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — commits do not account for key decisions:\033[0m\n' >&2
  printf '%b' "$decision_findings" >&2
  # EACH FINDING ABOVE ALREADY STATES WHICH OF THE TWO STATES THE COMMIT IS IN, and this
  # summary must not contradict it. It previously told every author to ADD a trailer,
  # including the ones whose trailer was present and merely malformed — a remedy naming a
  # state the reader is not in reads as the gate being broken, and the next step after that
  # is `--no-verify`. Do NOT restore a single undifferentiated instruction here.
  #
  # ‼ AND THE REMEDY MUST LIST EVERY FORM THE PATTERN ADMITS. This sentence named only
  # `D-YYYYMMDD-NN` after the undated `D-nnn` form was accepted, so it told an author their
  # own valid identifier was not an option — THE PATTERN-WIDENED-MESSAGE-NOT-UPDATED SHAPE,
  # FOURTH INSTANCE, INSIDE THE COMMIT THAT FIXED THE THIRD. Reported by r2-standard through
  # the escalation route. Whoever widens the pattern next: THE REMEDY TEXT IS PART OF THE
  # PATTERN. They are edited together or the gate lies about itself again.
  #
  # Only the bullets that actually fired are printed. Printing both on every refusal reads
  # as a legend, and a legend is where a reader stops distinguishing their case from the
  # other one — which is how the false-absence message survived two repairs.
  printf '\nRead DECISIONS.md.\n' >&2
  if grep -q 'neither updates DECISIONS.md' <<<"$decision_findings"; then
    printf '  • Trailer MISSING — amend to add an accepted trailer:\n' >&2
    printf '      Decision-Log: none            routine work, no decision\n' >&2
    printf '      Decision-Log: D-277           undated lane ledger ID\n' >&2
    printf '      Decision-Log: D-20260808-01   dated form (also R-)\n' >&2
    printf '      Decision-Log: d448            FLEET register — lowercase, NOT the same\n' >&2
    printf '                                    namespace as D-NNN. Do not substitute.\n' >&2
  fi
  if grep -q 'in a form this gate does not accept' <<<"$decision_findings"; then
    printf '  • Trailer PRESENT but not accepted — amend so the identifier is the WHOLE\n' >&2
    printf '    value; put the reasoning in the commit body. The accepted forms are\n' >&2
    printf '    printed beside your offending line above.\n' >&2
  fi
  if grep -q 'removes DECISIONS.md' <<<"$decision_findings"; then
    printf '  • The commit REMOVES the ledger. That is itself an unaccounted decision.\n' >&2
  fi
  printf 'If the commit made a new key decision, append the record and commit it instead.\n' >&2
  printf 'Do not reach for --no-verify. It is use-banned and this refusal is legible.\n' >&2
  printf 'If this refusal names a state you are not in, STOP and send it to the supervisor.\n\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi

if [[ -n "$findings" ]]; then
  printf '\n\033[1;31m✗ PUSH BLOCKED — possible secret in the commits being pushed:\033[0m\n' >&2
  printf '%b' "$findings" >&2
  # DO NOT re-add a bypass suggestion here. This text is what an AGENT reads at the
  # moment it is blocked and under pressure, and every fleet lane is bound by a
  # MUST-NOT-BYPASS rule — so the old wording told the reader to do the one thing it
  # must not do. It nearly mattered: a `git add -A` once swept a secret-bearing file
  # into a commit, push-fail refused it, and the lane that hit that decision point was
  # the one with no such rule recorded (core, 20331bba). A gate that advertises its own
  # disable switch is training the operator to use it.
  printf '\nFix: remove the secret, rewrite the commit, push again.\n' >&2
  printf 'If you believe this is a false positive: STOP AND REPORT IT. Do not bypass.\n\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi

# preserve any pre-existing repo-local hook
hookdir="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks 2>/dev/null)"
if [[ -x "$hookdir/pre-push.local" ]]; then exec "$hookdir/pre-push.local" "$@"; fi
exit 0
